2. CONSISTENCY WITH ImageUploadField.tsx's HARD-BLOCK ERROR (T-002)
=========================================================================

Compared field-by-field:
  - role="alert"                         — same in both
  - TriangleAlert icon, aria-hidden=true  — same in both
  - red text, not color-only (icon+text) — same in both
  - icon inherits currentColor, no own color class — same in both
  - flex items-start gap-{2|2} layout    — same shape (gap-2 vs the field
                                            version's gap-2 too)
  - text-sm (header) vs text-[11px] (field) — deliberately different scale,
    appropriate: this is a page-level message, the field one is a per-input
    micro-error. Not an inconsistency — matches how ImageUploadField's own
    two states (11px field errors) differ in scale from page-level copy
    elsewhere in this codebase.
VERDICT: one error language, correctly reused, not reinvented.

=========================================================================
3. PLACEMENT / DISCOVERABILITY (item 2 of the dispatch)
=========================================================================

Grepped all six client files: exactly one <SettingsHeader> per file, and
its onSave is the ONLY call site of that page's save() — no second Save
button, no floating/sticky action bar duplicating it. The admin shell's
`sticky` class (admin/(dashboard)/layout.tsx:43) is on the sidebar <aside>
only — SettingsHeader itself is not sticky and scrolls with page content.

Consequence: since Save can only be clicked from inside the header, the
admin is necessarily already scrolled to the header at the moment they
click it (the button isn't reachable otherwise). The error therefore
renders directly below the control that was just activated, inside the
same viewport region the user's eyes/pointer already occupy — this holds
even on the two longest pages (HomepageSettingsClient, 175 lines / three
ObjectListEditors; WhoWeAreClient, 183 lines / leader CRUD) precisely
because scrolling back to the header is already a precondition of
triggering save() at all, not a new burden T-012 introduces.

Agree with /lead-dev's choice not to duplicate the error into
ObjectListEditor: a save failure (auth/collision/delete-guard) is a
whole-page outcome, and HomepageSettingsClient alone has three separate
ObjectListEditor instances — repeating the identical string next to each
would misattribute a page-level failure to specific unrelated lists.

role="alert" is a live region — announced on mount to assistive tech
without requiring focus to move, matching the ImageUploadField precedent
exactly (same conditional-mount pattern). No focus-shift or scroll-into-
view was added, and none is needed given the above.

